<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Decree of Canopus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/238-bce">238 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Canopus Stone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Decree of Canopus is a trilingual inscription of Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a>, demotic and Greek, that records an assembly of priests held at Canopus in March 238 BCE in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes">Ptolemy III Euergetes</a>, Queen Berenice and Princess Berenice. It lists the king's gifts to the temples, the return of divine statues carried off by Cambyses, the suppression of native Egyptian revolts and grain imported in a year of low flood, deifies the dead princess, and introduces a calendar of 365 and a quarter days, a reform that failed until Augustus. Copies have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> in 1866 and elsewhere since, and its many signs helped decipher hieroglyphs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/decree-of-canopus.jpg" alt="Decree of Canopus" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 𐰇𐱅𐰚𐰤 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanopos_Kararnamesi_detay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
